# The vendored Matter bridge core's own Kconfig.
#
# Derived from C:\ncs\v3.4.0\nrf\applications\matter_bridge\src\core\Kconfig
# (NCS v3.4.0, LicenseRef-Nordic-5-Clause). The four BRIDGE_* symbols and the
# migration menu below are upstream's, name for name and default for default -
# bridge_manager.h and bridge_storage_manager.cpp read them by name, so the
# values are not ours to invent.
#
# WHAT WAS REMOVED, AND WHY IT IS REMOVED RATHER THAN SET TO n. Upstream ends
# with `if BRIDGED_DEVICE_BT` and a "Bluetooth bridge configuration" menu of
# eleven BRIDGE_BT_* symbols - scan windows, connection intervals, recovery
# timeouts. Every one of them configures the BLE-CENTRAL provider that
# src/matter/README.md says was deliberately left behind, and the sources that
# would read them are not in this repository. Sourcing the menu and setting the
# choice to n would leave eleven dead symbols in every Matter .config and one
# `if` on a symbol (BRIDGED_DEVICE_BT) that nothing in this tree defines.
#
# THIS IS NOT THE SAME BLUETOOTH AS CONFIG_BT. Matter commissions over BLE and
# CONFIG_BT arrives as a Kconfig default from connectedhomeip's Kconfig.defaults
# whatever this file says - see matter.conf's long note on the subject, and
# radiant/Kconfig's `depends on !BT || RADIANT_BACKEND_NRF_GATE_MPSL`.
#
# Sourced from apps/dongle_thread/Kconfig INSIDE `if CHIP`, for the same reason
# connectedhomeip's own files are: this application must still configure in a
# workspace with no Matter in it, and the five existing coexistence arms must
# not gain symbols.

menu "Matter bridge core (vendored from NCS matter_bridge)"

config BRIDGE_MAX_DYNAMIC_ENDPOINTS_NUMBER
	int "Maximum dynamic endpoints"
	default 16
	help
	  Maximum number of endpoints supported by the bridge.

	  THIS NUMBER IS ONE OF THREE THAT MUST AGREE, and the other two are
	  CHIP_DEVICE_CONFIG_DYNAMIC_ENDPOINT_COUNT (src/matter/chip_project_config.h)
	  and RADIANT_MATTER_MAX_ENDPOINTS (radiant/src/bridge/radiant_matter.h).
	  src/matter/bridge_core_asserts.cpp static_asserts the pair that C++ can
	  see. Trap 15: too small does not fail, it produces an Nth bridged
	  endpoint with no Descriptor cluster.

config BRIDGE_MAX_BRIDGED_DEVICES_NUMBER
	int "Maximum bridged devices"
	default 16
	help
	  Maximum number of physical non-Matter devices supported by the bridge.

	  One ANT+ sensor is one of these; the endpoints it announces are the
	  symbol above. radiant_binding.h's RADIANT_BINDING_MAX is 8, so this
	  ceiling is not the binding one.

config BRIDGE_MAX_BRIDGED_DEVICES_NUMBER_PER_PROVIDER
	int "Maximum endpoints per provider"
	default 2
	help
	  Maximum number of endpoints paired to one non-Matter provider device.

	  UPSTREAM'S 2 IS ALMOST CERTAINLY TOO SMALL FOR THIS PRODUCT and is left
	  at the upstream value here on purpose: one heart-rate binding announces
	  four derived booleans plus battery, which is five endpoints on one
	  provider. Raising it is package E4's call to make with the provider in
	  front of it, not E3's to make blind. Nothing in this repository
	  constructs a provider yet, so nothing hits the limit yet.

config BRIDGE_AGGREGATOR_ENDPOINT_ID
	int "Aggregator endpoint ID"
	default 1
	help
	  ID of the endpoint implementing Aggregator device type functionality.

	  1, because that is what default_zap/bridge.zap describes. Changing this
	  without regenerating the ZAP produces an aggregator id that no endpoint
	  in the data model has.

menu "Migration options"

config BRIDGE_MIGRATE_PRE_2_7_0
	bool "Migrate pre-2.7.0 data"
	help
	  Enable migration of bridged device data stored in the old scheme (pre-nRF SDK 2.7.0).

	  Off, and it stays off: this application has never stored a bridged
	  device in any scheme, so there is nothing to migrate from.

config BRIDGE_MIGRATE_VERSION_1
	bool "Migrate version 1 data"
	default y
	help
	  Enable migration of bridged device data stored in version 1 of the new scheme.

	  Left at upstream's default rather than turned off. It is read by
	  bridge_storage_manager.cpp, which is vendored verbatim, and a
	  first-boot device has no version-1 data to find - so the cost is code
	  size in a path that runs once and finds nothing.

endmenu

endmenu
